### Description
`record {| int a; never b?; |}` denotes exactly the same shapes as `record {| int a; |}` (the optional `never` field only forbids a field named `b`), but nutcracker treats it as not a subtype: assignment is rejected at compile time, and a runtime type test on a mutable value returns `false`. The readonly path gets it right (`true`).

**Spec:**
- §5.4.2 record type descriptor: "if ? is present, then the constraint is that if the mapping value shape has a field shape with the specified field-name, then its value shape must be a member of the specified type-descriptor".
- §5.7.4: "No value ever belongs to the never".
- §5.1: "a type S is a subtype of type T if the set of shapes denoted by S is a subset of the set of shapes denoted by T." So R3 ⊆ R2 (they are equivalent).
- §5.3: a mutable structured value belongs to a type iff its inherent type is a subtype of that type, so `v is R2` must be `true`.
- The spec relies on this idiom itself (included-record-param: a field descriptor "that has type never and is optional, and so disallows a field with that name").

### Steps to Reproduce
Compile-time:
```ballerina
import ballerina/io;

type R2 record {| int a; |};
type R3 record {| int a; never b?; |};

public function main() {
    R3 r3 = {a: 1};
    R2 r2 = r3;
    io:println(r2); // @output {"a":1}
}
```
Actual:
```
error[SEMANTIC_ERROR]: incompatible type: expected {| a: int, never... |}, got {| a: int, b: never, never... |}
```
(note the type is also printed as `b: never` without `?`).

Runtime:
```ballerina
import ballerina/io;

type R2 record {| int a; |};
type R3 record {| int a; never b?; |};

public function main() {
    R3 r3 = {a: 1};
    any v = r3;
    io:println(v is R2); // @output true
    R3 & readonly r4 = {a: 1};
    any w = r4;
    io:println(w is R2); // @output true
}
```
Actual: `false` then `true`. Expected: `true`, `true`.

### Affected Version(s)
`main` @ fb67e6e53730b6d658883c35937e01b8a183ca9a

### OS, DB, other environment details and versions
Linux, built with `go run ./cli/cmd run <file.bal>`

### Related area
Type checker / semtypes (record types, subtyping)

### Related issue(s) (optional)
None found.
